<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg" data-parallax="0.15"><img src="/assets/img/photos/consultation.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consultation-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consultation.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="682" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/our-appraisal-services/">Services</a></li><li aria-current="page">Appraisal Demand Representation</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Your Appointed Appraiser When You Invoke the Clause</h1><p class="lede">When you and your carrier agree the loss is covered but can’t agree on the number, the appraisal clause offers a way to settle the amount. We step in as the appraiser you name.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="hero-facts"><li><small>Good fit</small><b>Coverage accepted, price disputed</b></li><li><small>Decides</small><b>Amount of loss only</b></li><li><small>Award</small><b>Any two of three signatures</b></li></ul></div></section><div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><h2>Start with the words in your policy</h2>
<p>Appraisal in Mississippi comes from the policy itself. There’s no special state appraisal statute to lean on, so the clause printed in your homeowners, commercial or MWUA wind policy sets the rules. Most clauses look alike, but the details — who can demand it, how long each side has to act, who pays the umpire — vary by form and by carrier.</p>
<p>Before a demand goes out, we read the clause with you and flag anything unusual: a requirement that the demand go by a specific delivery method, language letting the carrier still contest coverage after the award, or conditions such as a sworn proof of loss or an examination under oath that may need to come first.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Written demand.</strong> Either party usually triggers appraisal in writing. A phone call to the adjuster generally isn’t enough.</li>
<li><strong>Appointment window.</strong> Each side names a competent, independent appraiser, often within 20 days of the demand.</li>
<li><strong>Umpire selection.</strong> The two appraisers try to agree on an umpire, often within 15 days. If they can’t, many clauses let either side ask a court of record to appoint one.</li>
<li><strong>Award.</strong> Agreement by any two of the three — both appraisers, or one appraiser and the umpire — sets the amount of loss.</li>
<li><strong>Costs.</strong> Each side typically pays its own appraiser and splits the umpire’s fee.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How the process usually unfolds</h2>
<h3>Demand and appointment</h3>
<p>Once you decide to proceed, the demand letter identifies the claim, the disputed amount and your appraiser. We help you draft it so it’s clear and tracks the clause. The carrier then names its own appraiser. If the carrier misses its window, put that delay in writing and raise it with a Mississippi attorney.</p>
<h3>Umpire and inspection</h3>
<p>The two appraisers trade names for an umpire and look for someone with no ties to either side. We inspect the property — roof, interior, contents, whatever is in dispute — and build our own estimate from measurements and current local pricing, not from the carrier’s numbers.</p>
<h3>Working toward the award</h3>
<p>Most gaps narrow once two appraisers put their estimates side by side and walk through them item by item. Whatever still differs goes to the umpire. The award states the amount of loss, often split into replacement cost and actual cash value, and is signed by two of the three.</p>
<div class="callout"><b class="callout-t">Deadlines keep running</b><p>Appraisal doesn’t automatically pause every deadline in your policy. Mississippi’s general limitation period for many contract claims is three years, but your policy wording and your facts can matter. Confirm your deadline with a Mississippi attorney before relying on any date.</p></div>
<h2>Questions appraisal won’t answer</h2>
<p>Appraisal decides what the covered loss is worth. It generally doesn’t decide whether the loss is covered, whether an exclusion applies or whether the carrier acted in bad faith. Those are legal questions for counsel or a court. We’re not a law firm and not a public adjuster, and we’ll tell you plainly when your dispute looks like a coverage fight rather than a pricing fight.</p>
<p>It also may not fit if the damage isn’t documented yet, or if the gap between the two estimates is smaller than the cost of the process. The <a href="/claim-calculator-suite/appraisal-readiness-checkup/">appraisal readiness checkup</a> walks through those questions in a few minutes.</p>
<h2>What to gather, and what you get back</h2>
<p>Bring the full policy with declarations and endorsements, the carrier’s estimate and payment letters, any contractor bids, and photos from before and after the storm or leak. A timeline helps too; the <a href="/claim-calculator-suite/claim-calendar-builder/">claim calendar builder</a> can sketch one from your dates.</p>
<p>In return you get a documented estimate, updates as the appraisers talk, and a plain explanation of the award when it’s signed. For more background, read the <a href="/claim-advice/mississippi-appraisal-clause-guide/">Mississippi appraisal clause guide</a>. If both sides need a neutral third party instead, see our <a href="/our-appraisal-services/neutral-umpire-services/">umpire services</a>.</p>
<h2>Available across Mississippi</h2>
<p>Appraisal Demand Representation is available statewide. Inspections are scheduled around your timeline in places such as <a href="/counties-and-cities/jackson-hinds-county/">Jackson</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/gulfport-harrison-county/">Gulfport</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/biloxi-harrison-county/">Biloxi</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/hattiesburg-forrest-county/">Hattiesburg</a>.</p>
<h2>Appraisal Demand Representation questions</h2><div class="faqs"><details><summary>Can my carrier ignore a written appraisal demand in Mississippi?</summary><p>It depends on the clause. Many policies let either party demand appraisal, but some add conditions or make it optional, so ask a Mississippi attorney how your specific wording is likely to be read.</p></details><details><summary>How long does a Mississippi appraisal usually take?</summary><p>A narrow roof dispute can wrap up in weeks, while a large storm or fire loss takes longer. Timing depends mostly on how fast appraisers are named, how quickly an umpire is agreed and how complex the damage is.</p></details><details><summary>Does the appraisal award settle coverage questions too?</summary><p>Generally no. The award sets the amount of loss, while coverage, exclusions and any bad-faith questions stay separate legal issues.</p></details><details><summary>Do I still deal with my adjuster during appraisal?</summary><p>Often yes, for payments and paperwork. The amount in dispute, though, is worked out between the two appraisers and, if needed, the umpire.</p></details></div>
